@endnode

@node ABOUT "What it does"
@{b}What it does@{ub}

Drag a window by its title bar until the @{b}pointer@{ub} touches a
screen edge or a corner. A frame shows where the window will land;
let go and it fills that half or that quarter of the screen - the
behaviour Windows and macOS users take for granted, on systems that
never had it.

Two things it is careful about:

@{b}Docks are never covered.@{ub} A dock or panel along an edge is
detected and the space it occupies is left alone, with a little
breathing room, so a snapped window sits beside it rather than on
top of it. This works with docks that float away from the edge too.

@{b}Windows share their edge.@{ub} When two windows end up side by
side, the seam between them can be dragged: the pointer becomes a
double arrow and both windows are resized at once, so half and half
becomes sixty and forty. Neither window can be squeezed out of
existence.

It runs as a commodity, so it starts with the system and Exchange
enables, disables or removes it like any other.

@endnode

@node INSTALL "Installing it"
@{b}Installing it@{ub}

Double-click the @{b}Install@{ub} icon. That is all: the installer
recognises the system it is running on, proposes the matching build,
and asks before doing anything.

What it puts where:

  @{b}LIBS:edgesnap.library@{ub}   everything EdgeSnap knows how to do
  @{b}C:EdgeSnap@{ub}              the commodity you interact with
  @{b}ENVARC:EdgeSnap.prefs@{ub}   the settings, commented, changing nothing
  one line in @{b}S:User-Startup@{ub}   so it is simply there at every boot
  @{b}SYS:Prefs/EdgeSnap@{ub}      the settings window
  the manual, where you say

On AROS the commodity goes to @{b}SYS:WBStartup/EdgeSnap@{ub} with its
icon instead, and Wanderer starts it at every boot: a Run line in
S:User-Startup would open a console window for it there and keep it
open. S:User-Startup is not touched.

On AROS for the Raspberry Pi there is no Installer to go through:
Install does the work itself, as it does on any AROS whose own
Installer cannot copy files. One requester says what goes where and
asks; the manual goes to SYS:Documentation, or SYS:Locale/Help where
there is none; and no settings file is written, since the preferences
window writes one when you press Save.

@{b}Updating@{ub} is installing again. The running copy is stopped,
the old library is let go of, and the new one takes over - no reboot,
and nothing for you to type. On the Raspberry Pi the old library cannot
be let go of yet, so a new one takes over at the next boot. Your
settings are left alone: the commented template is only offered when
ENVARC:EdgeSnap.prefs does not exist yet, and a file already there is
never touched.

To remove it: delete the two files above and the line between
@{b};BEGIN EdgeSnap@{ub} and @{b};END EdgeSnap@{ub} in S:User-Startup.
On AROS delete the library and the two files in SYS:WBStartup.

@endnode

@node USING "Using it"
@{b}Using it@{ub}

@{b}Snapping@{ub}
Drag a window's title bar until the pointer touches an edge or a
corner, then release. The zones are: left and right edges for halves,
the four corners for quarters, the top edge to maximise.

A corner is the corner itself. Anywhere along a side edge gives the
half, even with the title bar right under the screen's own; for a
quarter, push the pointer on into the corner, where the screen stops
it. The top edge is the screen's own title bar, for the same reason: a
window that sits at the top is not maximised the moment it is touched.
If you would rather have longer corners, and a top edge that reaches
into the screen, the Corner size setting gives them back. Preferences
saved by EdgeSnap 0.4 or earlier keep the corner size they had, 4: set
it to 0 for the corner itself.

If a window is already snapped to one side, snapping another to the
other side gives it the space that is actually left, not a rigid
half - so a sixty/forty split stays sixty/forty.

@{b}Windows that may not leave the screen@{ub}
A drag snaps where the pointer reaches the edge. Whether a dragged
window may go on past the edge is the system's own setting: in IControl
on MorphOS and AROS, in the GUI preferences on AmigaOS 4 (off-screen
dragging). Where it may not, the window stops at the edge and holds
the pointer back where it has the title bar. Push the mouse on a little
further against the edge and EdgeSnap counts the pointer as being
there: the frame comes up and stays while you hold. For a corner, push
on into both edges, towards the corner. The zones still answer more
directly when the window can go on.

MorphOS as delivered keeps windows on the screen, so the first
installation there chooses IControl's "allow positioning beyond the
screen limits", says so on a page of its own, and MorphOS takes it up
at once. An update leaves the setting as it finds it. AROS and
AmigaOS 4 let a window go past the edge as delivered, unless someone
turned it off.

To change it either way, use the box @{b}Windows can move off-screen@{ub}
in EdgeSnap's preferences, in the section named after the system, or
the system's own editor: IControl, or GUI on AmigaOS 4. On MorphOS,
IControl's key, Control as delivered, turns the setting round while it
is held.

@{b}The seam@{ub}
Where two snapped windows meet there is nothing to see until the
pointer comes near: then the join lights up in the same colour as the
preview frame. Press on it and drag, and both windows are resized
together; on AmigaOS 4 and MorphOS the pointer becomes a double arrow
and the windows follow as you drag, on AROS only the line follows and
the windows change when you let go, the way AROS moves its own
windows. Dragging far towards one side stops where a window would
become smaller than it allows - that is a limit, not a fault. Three or
four windows that share one line share one seam.

@{b}Hotkeys@{ub}
  @{b}ctrl alt cursor left@{ub}    snap the active window left
  @{b}ctrl alt cursor right@{ub}   snap it right
      Press the same one again and the window takes two thirds of the
      screen, again and it takes one third, again and it is back to a
      half. Turn that off with @{b}CYCLESIZES@{ub} if you would rather
      have halves only.
  @{b}ctrl alt cursor up@{ub}      maximise it
  @{b}ctrl alt cursor down@{ub}    put it back where it was
  @{b}ctrl alt c@{ub}              put it in the middle, at the size it has
      Nothing about the middle says how big a window should be, so it
      keeps its own size; one larger than the screen area is cut down
      to it. @{b}ctrl alt cursor down@{ub} puts it back as usual.
  @{b}ctrl alt z@{ub}              open the selector under the pointer
      A small palette that is the map of the screen: nine cells, each
      where the window would go, with a miniature of its zone. Point
      and click, or move with the cursor keys and press Return; the
      middle cell is the centre and the bottom middle puts the window
      back. Esc, the right button, the hotkey again or a click anywhere
      else closes it. It acts on the window that was active when it
      opened.
  @{b}ctrl alt d@{ub}              print a window dump, to diagnose dock detection

@{b}Changing the settings@{ub}
Open @{b}SYS:Prefs/EdgeSnap@{ub} - the installer puts it there, with
the rest of the system's preferences. Change what you like and press
Save; EdgeSnap follows immediately, without being restarted. Use
applies the change for this session only, Cancel changes nothing.

The window offers every setting listed under
@{" Settings " link SETTINGS}, each with the range it accepts, so
nothing you can choose there can be refused. It also has the
system's own "Windows can move off-screen", in a section named after
the system (see above): it is kept in the system's preferences, not in
EdgeSnap's, and the system follows it at once.

@{b}Getting past the zones@{ub}
If you want to drag a window right up to the edge without it
snapping, set @{b}BYPASSQUAL@{ub} (see @{" Settings " link SETTINGS})
and hold that key while you drag.

@endnode

%%SETTINGS%%

@node SHELL "From the Shell"
@{b}From the Shell@{ub}

  @{b}EdgeSnap@{ub}
      Start it. Settings come from ENV: or ENVARC:, and anything you
      add on the command line wins over the file.

  @{b}EdgeSnap ZONES=halves EDGEPX=24@{ub}
      Start it with those settings for this run.

  @{b}EdgeSnap QUIT@{ub}
      Stop the copy that is running, and start nothing. This is what
      the installer uses to replace a running EdgeSnap without a
      reboot. Starting EdgeSnap a second time by accident is simply
      refused; only QUIT stops it.

  @{b}SYS:Prefs/EdgeSnap OFFSCREEN QUERY|ON|OFF@{ub}
      Ask or set whether windows can move off-screen, the system's
      own setting, without opening the window. QUERY returns 0 when
      they can and 5 when they cannot.

Run from a Shell rather than with @{b}Run >NIL:@{ub} (on AROS
@{b}Run >NIL: <NIL:@{ub}) and it prints what it decides, drag by drag -
useful when something does not behave as expected.

Settings are re-read while it runs: whatever writes
@{b}ENV:EdgeSnap.prefs@{ub} - the preferences window, an editor, a
script - is noticed at once.

@endnode

@node LIBRARY "For programmers"
@{b}For programmers@{ub}

@{b}Two version numbers, and why@{ub}
EdgeSnap is 0.42: a beta, complete in what it does and tested on real
MorphOS hardware, on a Raspberry Pi 400 with AROS, and on AmigaOS 4 and
AROS x86_64 under emulation.
@{b}edgesnap.library@{ub} says %%LIBVER%%, and that is not a mistake: a
library's version is its interface, not its product. It reached 2 when
the seam was added to the API, and the revision climbs with every build
meant to replace an earlier one, because that is how the installer
decides whether to replace it.

While EdgeSnap is below 1.0, treat the library's interface as @{b}not
frozen@{ub}: calls are only ever added at the end, never moved or
removed, but names and arguments may still change if a better shape
turns up. If you build on it now, say which revision you built against,
and check lib_Revision before using a call added after 2.2.

The behaviour lives in @{b}edgesnap.library@{ub}, not in the
commodity. The commodity is a client like any other: it contributes
raw input and draws the preview frame and the seam, and every decision
happens inside the library. So another program can ask for the same
things:

  @{b}Snapping@{ub}
  ESnap_SnapWindow           place a window in a zone
  ESnap_UnsnapWindow         put it back where it was
  ESnap_QueryWindow          which zone a window is in
  ESnap_ExcludeWindow        keep a window out of snapping

  @{b}The seam between two windows@{ub}
  ESnap_QueryDivider         the seam of the layout
  ESnap_QueryDividerAt       the seam nearest a point
  ESnap_MoveDivider          move the seam, resizing both sides
  ESnap_MoveDividerAt        move a named seam

  @{b}Screen and windows (2.5, 2.6, 2.11)@{ub}
  ESnap_QueryScreenArea      where windows may go, docks left clear
  ESnap_QueryWorkAreas       the same, with an id that outlives a
                             screen-mode change
  ESnap_QueryWindows         the windows of a screen, with their state
  ESnap_QueryGeneration      a number that moves when anything did
  ESnap_QueryWindowSerial    an identity for a window that its
  ESnap_FindWindow           address cannot give
  ESnap_PlaceWindow          put a window in a rectangle
  ESnap_PlaceWindowsA        put several at once, a whole layout

  @{b}Clients and groups (2.10, 2.12)@{ub}
  ESnap_RegisterClient       one client owns the interactive engine,
  ESnap_UnregisterClient     any number may own layouts
  ESnap_CreateGroup          windows a layout client looks after,
  ESnap_GroupAddWindow       optionally locked against everyone
  ESnap_GroupRemoveWindow    else, the commodity's drag included
  ESnap_QueryGroupOf
  ESnap_DeleteGroup

  @{b}Running it@{ub}
  ESnap_Enable               switch snapping, or a layout, on or off
  ESnap_SetOptionsA          the settings, as tags
  ESnap_IgnoreWindows        a frontend's own windows
  ESnap_FeedInput            a frontend's input, and what it did
  ESnap_FeedMotion
  ESnap_ResetInput

The library validates every window handle on every call, so a window
that has been closed gives an error rather than a crash. The contract
of every call, with what it returns and from which revision it exists,
is in @{b}include/edgesnap.h@{ub} in the source repository.

@{b}esnaptest@{ub}, in this package's drawer for each system (os4, mos,
aros64 or aarch64), is a small client that opens the library and
drives the whole API: a worked example, and the quickest way to see
which revision a machine is really running.

@endnode

@node AUTHOR "Licence and author"
@{b}Licence and author@{ub}

EdgeSnap was written by @{b}Michele Dipace@{ub}
<michele.dipace@kaffeine.net>.

The icons are by @{b}Carlo Spadoni@{ub}, who drew them for EdgeSnap
and let me ship them with it.

It is distributed under the MIT licence: you may use, copy, change
and redistribute it, including in commercial work, as long as the
copyright notice and the licence text travel with it. It comes with
no warranty.

@{b}Source, and where to report what you find@{ub}
EdgeSnap is still a beta, and every report has made it better: the
docks it missed, the corners, the push against the edge all came from
people who wrote in. If it covers your dock, misses your seam, or draws
something odd, that is exactly the report worth having:

  https://github.com/kaffeine1/edgesnap/issues

The same place holds the source, the design notes and what was learned
the hard way on each system.

@endnode
